        "When a doctor says that a treatment has a “ten percent risk” of causing a side effect, what does the patient hear? For many people, the answer is: not what the doctor meant. Some imagine that the side effect will certainly happen but only mildly; others assume that ten percent is so small that it does not apply to them. Research on how people understand numbers in medicine has shown that {u:(1):the same information can lead to very different decisions depending on how it is presented}.",
        "Consider two ways of describing the same surgery. In one, patients are told that ninety out of a hundred people survive the operation. In the other, they are told that ten out of a hundred die. The facts are identical, yet in many studies, far more people choose surgery when they hear the first version. Even experienced doctors are not immune to this effect. When the same choices were described to groups of physicians in “survival” or “death” terms, their preferences shifted in much the same way as those of the public.",
        "Another common source of confusion is the difference between relative and absolute risk. Suppose a new drug reduces the chance of a heart attack from two in a hundred to one in a hundred. A report may describe this as “cutting the risk in half,” which sounds dramatic. But for any single patient, the chance of benefiting is only one in a hundred. {u:(2):Both descriptions are true, but they leave very different impressions.} Patients who hear only the relative figure tend to overestimate how much they will gain, and may accept side effects that they would refuse if they understood the absolute numbers.",
        "Researchers have tested several ways of making risks clearer. One of the most effective is to use natural frequencies — for example, “out of a thousand people like you, about thirty will have this side effect” — rather than percentages or probabilities. Pictures can also help: a grid of a hundred small figures, with a few colored in, allows patients to see at a glance how many people are affected. Such tools are especially useful for patients who find numbers difficult, but studies show that even highly educated people understand risks better when they are presented in this way.",
